OUT = r"E:\c++\exchange-simulator\AstraX_CPP_Technical_Viva_60_QA_Updated.docx"

sections = [
("Architecture and execution flow", [
("Give me a precise 60-second architecture overview. Which claims can you prove from source?", "AstraX is a C++20 in-memory matching engine surrounded by Python tooling. The core path is `MatchingEngine::submit` → matching against the `OrderBook` → `Fill` records; `OrderBook` uses ordered price maps, per-level intrusive queues, an order-ID index, and pooled nodes. The repo also contains a pybind11 module, a fixed-width binary codec, independent-symbol batching, replay/agent/RL code, and a WebSocket dashboard. I would describe the dashboard and AI as supporting tools, not as part of the matching hot path. Source confirms code exists; it does not prove every documented feature is production complete."),
 ("Trace one incoming limit order from the Python caller to a trade and explain ownership at each step.", "pybind11 exposes `MatchingEngine::submit(Order&)`; the C++ engine first stores the input timestamp, dispatches by order type, and calls `match_limit`. That checks FOK liquidity when requested, then `match_against_book` walks the opposite side’s best levels and emits fills at maker prices. Any GTC remainder is copied into a pooled `OrderNode` through `OrderBook::add`; the original Python-bound `Order` is separate from the resting copy. I would verify exact binding conversion and lifetime semantics for the pinned pybind version before relying on details beyond the source signature."),
 ("What is the central invariant of price-time priority here?", "At a given price, orders are appended to `PriceLevel`’s intrusive FIFO and matching consumes `front()`. Price priority comes from `std::map` comparators: bids use `greater<Price>`, asks use `less<Price>`. Thus the best bid is the highest bid and the best ask is the lowest ask; FIFO is only within one level. The invariant assumes orders enter the queue in accepted arrival order, and current code does not sort by the order’s supplied timestamp."),
 ("What exactly does the engine mean by timestamp, and is it the source of time priority?", "`Order::timestamp` is documented as nanoseconds since epoch and is serialized, but the book queue is insertion ordered rather than timestamp ordered. Matching stamps fills from `current_ts_`, set from the submitted order; iceberg refill increments `current_ts_`. A delayed order carrying an old timestamp still joins at the tail. For deterministic exchange semantics, I would assign a sequencer timestamp on acceptance and keep external event time distinct from queue priority time."),
 ("Which operations are O(1), O(log P), or O(N), where P is price levels and N is resting orders?", "Best bid/ask access is O(1) through `map::begin`; price-level insertion or removal is O(log P), while adding/canceling also uses average O(1) hash lookup. Matching is O(number of consumed orders × map-level transitions), approximately O(K + L log P) for K maker orders across L levels because empty-level removal touches the map. FOK pre-check scans relevant levels, and auction candidate evaluation is substantially more expensive: it loops over candidate prices and rescans levels, O(P²) in the straightforward implementation."),
 ("Why is price represented as an integer instead of a floating-point value? What is still missing?", "`Price` is `int64_t`, with a comment that values are scaled by 10,000. Integer ticks avoid binary floating-point comparison errors in ordering and matching. The engine does not enforce a tick size, scale, nonnegative prices, or overflow-safe arithmetic, and the Python replay’s `price_scale` can create unexpected conversion behavior. A production contract should specify currency, scale, tick, range, and validation at ingress."),
 ("What does the build actually compile, and what should you avoid claiming?", "Top-level CMake requests C++20, enables tests, and adds `engine` and `bindings`. Engine CMake fetches GoogleTest 1.15.2 and Google Benchmark 1.9.1; bindings fetch pybind11 2.13.6 and require Python development components. There are 15 listed C++ test translation units and two benchmark targets. The repository scan shows no pinned Python lockfile for all optional packages and no CI/deployment definition in the listed tree, so I would not claim a fully reproducible production build without exercising those configurations."),
 ("Where are the trust boundaries between components?", "External orders can enter through the Python binding or the dashboard TCP handler’s binary decoder. The decoder checks exact frame size, version, and message type, but does not validate enum domains or economic invariants before the order reaches the engine. The WebSocket handler also accepts JSON commands and integer limits with little validation. I would make one validated command boundary, enforce identity/quantity/price/TIF constraints there, and keep internal matching APIs on already-validated types."),
 ("Does the README prove the dashboard and the claimed data sources work end to end?", "No. README and REPORT describe replay, dashboard, AI, and regime search, but code inspection only establishes implementation presence. The dashboard imports the built extension from `build/bindings`, starts WebSocket and TCP servers, and calls LM Studio synchronously from handlers; no deployment orchestration is evident in the enumerated repo. I would separately run the documented flows and report tested configurations rather than infer success from documentation."),
 ("What state is owned by `MatchingEngine`, and what is the lifetime model?", "The engine owns its `OrderBook`, dormant stop-order vector, pegged-order metadata vector, current phase/time, and last trade price. The book owns price maps, the order-ID index, and an `ObjectPool<OrderNode>`; each pooled node contains an `Order`. This makes engine/book destruction responsible for all resting node storage, but `ObjectPool`’s arena releases raw chunks without walking outstanding objects, so current `OrderNode`’s trivial resource needs make that work while a future nontrivial node would need explicit destruction."),
 ("What would you change first to make the architecture safer without sacrificing the model?", "I would centralize order validation and make accepted order IDs unique across all live order states. Then I would fix lifecycle indexing for stop/pegged orders and define precise semantics for `StopLimit`, iceberg totals, and auction tie-breaks. I would put a single-writer sequencer around each symbol, make multi-asset routing safe, and add invariant/property tests before micro-optimizing. Each change should preserve a replayable event sequence so correctness can be audited."),
 ("How do you distinguish implemented behavior from design intent in an interview?", "I cite the concrete function and state what it actually does, then label missing behavior plainly. For example, `StopLimit` is present in the enum and stored as a dormant stop, but `check_stop_triggers` converts every trigger to `Market`; it does not become a limit. The code comment marks stop-limit future work, and I would not describe it as implemented until code and tests establish the contract."),
 ("If the process crashes after creating a fill but before broadcasting it, what recovery guarantees exist?", "The C++ engine is in-memory and emits fills as return values; the inspected matching path has no durable journal or transaction boundary. The dashboard broadcasts snapshots and recent activity separately, so a process crash can lose book state and fills. A production design needs a write-ahead event log or replicated sequencer, deterministic replay, and idempotent downstream publication; none should be claimed as present here."),
]),
("Matching semantics and algorithms", [
 ("Walk through a buy limit crossing two ask levels and state the execution prices.", "`match_against_book` repeatedly takes `best_ask()`, stops once its price exceeds the buy limit, and consumes the front maker order. Each `Fill.price` is `maker.price`, so a marketable buy at 105 against asks at 100 and 103 executes at 100 first and then 103, subject to available quantity. The incoming order’s `filled_quantity` increases after each fill; any GTC residual rests as a bid."),
 ("How does the FOK pre-check work, and what subtle correctness risks do you see?", "`available_quantity` scans crossing price levels and sums each level’s `total_quantity` before matching; `match_limit` rejects if that sum is below the requested total quantity. It does not mutate the book, so the intent is all-or-nothing. However, the accumulator is `Quantity` (`uint32_t`) and can wrap across levels; it also needs validation of pre-filled quantity and must compare against remaining quantity rather than blindly total quantity if partially filled incoming orders are legal. The check and execution are safe only under single-writer access to that book."),
 ("What is the distinction between IOC, FOK, and GTC in this implementation?", "GTC limit orders match what they can and rest any remaining quantity. IOC and FOK remainders are discarded; FOK first performs a liquidity pre-check, while IOC does not. Market orders call matching directly and never rest, regardless of TIF. API validation is weak, so nonsensical combinations are not rejected consistently; the contract should explicitly define supported type/TIF combinations."),
 ("Can a market order execute at a price worse than the user expected?", "Yes. `match_market` has no price guard and consumes all available opposing liquidity until the order is filled or the book empties. There is no market protection collar, maximum notional, or slippage limit. A safer interface can represent a marketable limit with a worst acceptable price and reject/expire the remainder beyond that bound."),
 ("Why does a partial maker fill preserve its queue position?", "The maker node remains in the same intrusive queue unless `is_filled()` becomes true. Its remaining quantity is reduced and the price level aggregate is decremented, so following orders do not jump ahead. This is the expected price-time behavior, assuming aggregate and per-order quantities remain consistent."),
 ("Name the state invariants you would assert after every match or cancel.", "For every level, `total_quantity` should equal the sum of each node’s remaining visible quantity; `order_count` should equal linked nodes; head/tail and next/prev links must be symmetric; each node’s side and price must agree with its containing map; each live ID must map to exactly one node. Per order, `0 <= filled_quantity <= quantity`; globally, each fill must reduce buyer and seller remaining quantities equally. These are excellent property-test and debug-assertion targets."),
 ("How is the uncross price selected, and what auction rule is missing?", "`uncross` forms the set of occupied bid and ask prices, computes executable volume as `min(buy_vol, sell_vol)` at each candidate, then breaks volume ties using smaller imbalance. If still tied, the first candidate encountered wins because no further tie-break is implemented; set iteration is ascending. Many auctions use a reference/last-trade price tie-break and explicit imbalance/direction rules. I would document this simplified rule and add tests for unresolved ties."),
 ("Does the auction preserve price-time priority when allocating the clearing quantity?", "It selects best bid and best ask levels, then consumes the front orders on each side, which preserves price priority and FIFO at each side while matching. It executes all fills at `best_price`, the chosen clearing price, even though each resting order has its own limit. It does not trigger stop orders, reprice pegs, or explicitly refill iceberg slices during uncross, so those interactions need a defined policy."),
 ("What happens when a limit order would cross but its `book_.add` fails?", "Matching still returns whatever fills already occurred; resting residual insertion is attempted but its boolean result is ignored. A pool allocation failure can silently drop a GTC remainder after accepting it. Also, map/hash allocations can throw rather than return false, and add is not exception-safe across node allocation, level insertion, and ID-index insertion. The API needs explicit accepted/rejected/partial status and strong rollback semantics."),
 ("How should duplicate order IDs be handled, and what does the code currently do?", "`OrderBook::add` does not reject a live duplicate ID; `order_map_[id]` overwrites the prior location while the old node remains linked. A later cancel then removes only the newer order, leaving an unindexed orphan that can still trade. IDs must be validated uniquely before any mutation, with cross-symbol uniqueness if routed globally, and rejected atomically. This is one of the highest-risk correctness defects visible in the source."),
 ("What are the numeric overflow and underflow hazards in matching?", "`Order::remaining()` subtracts unsigned 32-bit values without checking whether filled exceeds total; level aggregates and FOK sums are also 32-bit and can overflow. Auction volume/imbalance arithmetic is 32-bit. Malformed or duplicated orders can therefore violate assumptions and wrap quantities. Validate `filled_quantity <= quantity`, use checked or widened aggregate arithmetic, and cap accepted quantities based on explicit exchange limits."),
 ("How would you prove matching correctness beyond example-based tests?", "Build a slow reference matcher with simple vectors/maps and compare it to the optimized engine over generated command sequences. After each command, compare fills and canonicalized book state and check the invariants from the prior answer. Use deterministic seeds, shrinking/property-based generation, boundary quantities/prices, duplicate IDs, cancels after partial fills, and FOK insufficient-liquidity cases; record the seed and command trace on failure."),
 ("What is the semantic risk of using the taker’s timestamp as fill timestamp?", "`submit` sets `current_ts_` from the incoming order, and each fill gets that value, not a monotonic engine clock. Out-of-order or malicious client timestamps can make the trade tape go backward and can affect downstream replay. The engine should distinguish client event time from exchange-assigned receive/sequence time and use the latter for ordering and audit timestamps."),
]),
("Data structures memory and concurrency", [
 ("Why combine ordered maps, intrusive queues, a hash index, and an object pool?", "The ordered maps deliver best-price access and price-level ordering; intrusive doubly linked queues preserve FIFO and allow O(1) unlink once a node is known; the hash map makes ID cancellation average O(1); the object pool reduces per-order allocation churn. The trade-off is more invariants and more complicated failure handling than a standard container design. Performance claims need benchmark evidence under realistic level counts and cancellation patterns."),
 ("What does `alignas(64)` guarantee, and does it prove false sharing is gone?", "It requests at least 64-byte alignment for `Order` and `OrderNode`, which is often chosen to align hot objects to a cache line. It does not prove that 64 bytes is the hardware’s cache-line size, eliminate false sharing for adjacent fields, or improve locality; it may inflate memory footprint. Measure with hardware counters and consider separating frequently written per-thread state from read-mostly data."),
 ("Explain the allocation and object-lifetime mechanics of `ObjectPool<OrderNode>`.", "`SlabAllocator` obtains an arena chunk, splits it into aligned slots, links free slots using an embedded `FreeNode`, and returns a slot. `ObjectPool::create` placement-news the object; `destroy` calls its destructor and returns the slot to the free list. Alignment is rounded to `alignof(T)`. The allocator is not thread-safe, and callers must destroy every live object before reuse; pool teardown frees chunks without destructing outstanding objects."),
 ("What exception-safety issue exists in `OrderBook::add`?", "After acquiring a node, `add` mutates the price map/queue, then inserts into `order_map_`. If map or hash allocation throws partway through, the node and level may be left inconsistent or leaked from the pool. A robust implementation would reserve/index first where possible, use RAII guards, and commit links only after all potentially throwing work succeeds, with rollback for every intermediate state."),
 ("Is `MemoryPool<T, Capacity>` safe for arbitrary T?", "No. It offers raw storage and reuses the first bytes of each slot as a free-list index via reinterpret casts; it never constructs or destroys `T`, and its correctness depends on alignment, lifetime rules, and the pointer belonging to the pool. `deallocate` does not validate ownership, double-free, or current allocation state. It is a low-level fixed-slot storage exercise, not a general safe allocator API."),
 ("What is the actual usable capacity of `SpscRingBuffer<T, 4>`?", "Three elements. It reserves one slot to distinguish full from empty by comparing `next_tail == head`; the included test explicitly expects the fourth push to fail. That is a valid design if documented, but callers may assume the template capacity is usable capacity. Capacity 1 has zero usable slots, and the destructor also requires default construction and move assignment of T despite no such constraints being stated."),
 ("Explain the ring buffer’s acquire/release synchronization in producer-consumer terms.", "The producer constructs an element in its exclusive tail slot, then release-stores the new tail. The consumer acquire-loads tail before reading that published element, then release-stores head after destroying it. The producer acquire-loads head before reusing a slot, ensuring it observes consumer completion. Head and tail are single-writer fields under the SPSC contract; using multiple producers or consumers would break this reasoning."),
 ("Is `submit_parallel` truly thread-safe for simultaneous public calls?", "No thread-safety contract is enforced. The method writes shared `order_to_symbol_` and `books_` before launching tasks; concurrent external calls can race. It groups a batch by symbol and launches one async task per symbol, so tasks mutate distinct `MatchingEngine` objects, but shared map access and simultaneous access to the same engine outside the function remain concerns. A symbol actor/strand or explicit synchronization is needed."),
 ("Why does `submit_parallel` use an SPSC queue that is created and consumed by the same worker?", "The worker pushes its copied vector of orders into a local ring buffer and then pops them itself; there is no separate producer thread. That gives no concurrent handoff and adds queue operations/copies/moves without exploiting SPSC synchronization. The async task still gives parallelism across symbols. Either process the vector directly or connect a real producer/consumer boundary and benchmark both."),
 ("What happens if `submit_parallel` receives more than 1023 orders for one symbol?", "The ring buffer’s effective capacity is 1023 for `Capacity=1024`. The loop alternates pushing until full and draining until empty, so it should continue in batches, assuming each pushed order can be moved and each submit completes. The queue is local and single-threaded, so this is batching rather than pipelining; large batch behavior should have a test and avoid unnecessary per-task overhead."),
 ("How do `order_to_symbol_` and cancellation behave after fills or ID reuse?", "`submit` records every order ID to symbol before matching, and there is no removal on full fill or cancel. The map can grow without bound, and reusing an ID in another symbol redirects cancellation to the later symbol. Since `OrderBook::cancel` returns not found for a fully filled order, routing entries become stale. Maintain authoritative live-order routing and erase it atomically on terminal states; define behavior for IDs across all order types."),
 ("Name a cache-locality trade-off in the current order book.", "Intrusive nodes avoid a separate list allocation, and pooled slabs can improve allocation locality, but linked-list traversal still follows pointers and can miss cache lines. The price map and hash map add separate structures and indirections; `alignas(64)` may increase footprint. An alternative is flat price-level arrays or a radix/tree structure with contiguous order storage, but cancellation and dynamic price ranges complicate that design. Benchmark real workloads before choosing."),
 ("What is a lock-free algorithm, and can you safely call this whole engine lock-free?", "The ring buffer uses atomics and has a lock-free-style SPSC algorithm, though a formal progress claim also depends on atomic implementation and T’s operations. The matching engine, maps, pools, and multi-asset routing are not lock-free and generally are not thread-safe. The accurate claim is that a standalone SPSC queue is implemented with atomics; the exchange engine is not a lock-free matching engine."),
]),
("Protocol order types and integration", [
 ("Describe the binary order frame and its portability guarantees.", "The codec emits version and message-type bytes, then integer fields explicitly encoded little-endian, including reserved bytes; order frame size is computed as 56 bytes. This avoids serializing C++ struct padding and `alignas` layout. However, the code does not document endianness in a public protocol spec, validate enum ranges/reserved bytes, or add a checksum/length prefix; the TCP handler relies on fixed-size `readexactly` framing."),
 ("What decoder tests would you add for hostile or malformed input?", "Test every truncated and oversized length, wrong version/type, invalid side/type/TIF enum, nonzero reserved bytes, extreme signed prices, zero or inconsistent quantity, and fields that violate order invariants. Fuzz the decoder with arbitrary byte vectors under ASan/UBSan and assert it never crashes or emits an invalid accepted order. Exact-size checks prevent overread here, but validation is still incomplete."),
 ("How does the TCP feeder handle backpressure and partial network writes?", "The async feeder writes a complete encoded order frame and awaits `drain`, which provides asyncio transport backpressure. The server reads exactly one fixed-size order frame at a time and drains only when fill frames exist. TCP preserves byte order but not message boundaries, so `readexactly` is appropriate. There is no authentication, explicit connection limit, timeout, or malformed-client rate control in the inspected handler."),
 ("What does a stop order do when submitted, and what triggers it?", "Stop and StopLimit orders are appended to `stop_orders_` and do not rest in the price book. After a regular submission produces fills, the engine uses the last fill price and scans stops: buy stops trigger at or above stop price; sell stops at or below. Triggered orders are removed and matched as market orders, and cascades are rescanned. A stop submitted after the market already crossed its trigger is not immediately checked."),
 ("Why is the current `StopLimit` implementation a semantic defect?", "The enum comment says StopLimit becomes a limit when triggered, but the trigger handler unconditionally sets `triggered.type = Market` and invokes `match_market`. This can execute beyond the stop order’s intended limit and exposes the user to unbounded slippage. Preserve the limit price, convert to `Limit`, and route through `match_limit`; define TIF and re-trigger behavior and add price-protection tests."),
 ("How are pegged order prices computed, and what cancellation flaw follows?", "A buy peg references current best bid minus offset and is capped below best ask; a sell references best ask plus offset and is capped above best bid. On trades, `reprice_pegged_orders` cancels by ID and re-adds a limit copy. But public `cancel(id)` only searches the book, not the pegged metadata vector, so canceling a peg can remove its current book node while metadata remains and later reprice can resurrect it. Stop orders likewise cannot be canceled through the exposed API."),
 ("How does the iceberg expose hidden size and preserve priority?", "When an iceberg rests, `add_iceberg_to_book` puts only a visible slice into a node and stores the remainder in `hidden_quantity`. Once a slice is fully filled, the engine removes it and adds the next slice to the back of the price queue with a new timestamp, intentionally losing priority. The representation reuses quantity fields for slice-local quantity, so the original total and cumulative fills are not naturally preserved on the resting node; tests should cover partial fills, cancel, ID reuse, and total execution accounting."),
 ("What bug story can you tell from this code without inventing a past event?", "The source exposes a concrete defect: a live duplicate ID overwrites `order_map_[id]` while the earlier node remains linked. I can present it as an investigation: I traced cancellation through the hash index and intrusive queue, constructed two same-ID orders at different prices, and predicted that cancel would remove only the newest node while the older could still trade. The honest status from this repository review is that the defect and fix strategy are identified; this checkout does not prove I personally fixed or tested it."),
 ("Give the fix design and regression test for that duplicate-ID bug.", "Reject duplicate IDs before allocating or linking a node, and make ID uniqueness a shared invariant across the multi-asset router and dormant order collections. Add a regression that rests ID 7 at two prices, asserts the second submission is rejected without changing depth, cancels ID 7, and verifies no order with that ID can still trade. Also inject allocation failures around index insertion to prove rejection leaves no orphan. Only say ‘I fixed it’ after implementing and running those tests."),
 ("Does LOBSTER replay reconstruct an L3 book faithfully?", "No. `LobsterReplay.events()` parses new, cancel, execution, and halt event codes, but `generate()` yields only new limit orders. It does not apply partial/full cancels, visible/hidden executions, or trading halts to the simulator; therefore it is an order generator, not faithful L3 reconstruction. It also uses a configurable scale conversion and assumes CSV rows are ordered when breaking at end time."),
 ("What are two risks in the Hawkes generator’s thinning implementation?", "It estimates current intensity, samples an exponential wait from that total, then evaluates intensity after advancing time and accepts using the new/current rate divided by the old sampled rate. Standard thinning requires a valid dominating upper bound over the interval; self-exciting intensity can rise, so the old value may not dominate and the acceptance ratio can exceed one. Also `_intensity` uses histories and RNG internally, introducing random intensity noise rather than deterministic exponential kernel sums. I would validate the stochastic model against a reference implementation and statistical diagnostics before calling the synthetic flow calibrated."),
 ("What does the dashboard latency panel measure, and what does it omit?", "The server measures wall-clock duration around the Python-to-C++ `submit` binding using `perf_counter_ns`, retains 1000 samples, and reports discrete p50/p90/p95/p99 plus mean/min/max every 100 ticks. It omits network decode, serialization, queueing, broadcast, scheduling, and end-to-end client latency. Samples mix order types and do not capture histogram accuracy or coordinated omission; the metric is submission-call latency, not exchange latency in production."),
]),
("Testing performance security and improvement", [
 ("Which tests exist, and what does their existence fail to prove?", "CMake lists tests for types, pools, ring buffer, protocol, fuzz/stress matching, order book, matching, multi-asset, IOC/FOK, iceberg, stop, pegged, and auction behavior. Those filenames show intended coverage, not proof that all tests pass or cover all paths. I have not executed the suite for this document, and no coverage report was examined. Tests should be run on a clean build and results reported with compiler, configuration, and seed."),
 ("What is the value and limitation of the fuzz matching test in this repository?", "The randomized matching test can exercise many order interactions and uncover invariant violations missed by hand-written cases. Its strength depends on seed control, assertions, sequence length, and whether it checks against a reference model. A random test that merely submits orders without checking book conservation is weak; preserve failing seeds/traces and add shrinking to make failures reproducible."),
 ("How would you benchmark matching fairly?", "Separate workloads for empty-book inserts, crossing fills, deep sweeps, cancellation, same-level FIFO, FOK scans, and mixed order flow. Warm up, pin compiler/build flags and CPU conditions, report throughput and latency distributions, prevent dead-code elimination, and use enough repetitions. Google Benchmark targets exist, but benchmark source must be inspected and results collected before claiming a speedup; profiling scripts alone are not measurements."),
 ("Why can average latency hide an exchange-engine regression?", "Averages hide tail spikes caused by tree rebalancing, allocation, page faults, scheduler pauses, and unusually deep sweeps. Report p50/p90/p99 and maximum with enough samples, and distinguish engine-call latency from full path. For low-latency systems, tail behavior and workload composition often matter more than a single mean."),
 ("What security concerns do you see in the local dashboard service?", "Both servers bind to `localhost`, which limits default exposure, but the TCP endpoint accepts unauthenticated orders and WebSocket commands lack authorization or input bounds. A client can submit extreme/invalid data, send oversized/invalid JSON, trigger costly model calls, or request huge regime limits. If exposed beyond a trusted local demo, add authentication, schema validation, request caps, rate limits, and network isolation."),
 ("What failure mode can block the simulation loop when AI is unavailable?", "Regime archive catches `RuntimeError`, but chat analysis is invoked synchronously inside the async WebSocket handler and the LM client’s HTTP call can block up to the configured 30-second timeout. This can stall the event loop and delay simulation/broadcast. Offload model calls to a worker thread/task, set a smaller bounded timeout, and keep analysis queues isolated from market-state progression."),
 ("What does the project’s package metadata say about dependency reproducibility?", "`pyproject.toml` declares Python >=3.11 and NumPy, with optional ML/RL/dev groups, but version floors are broad and no Python lockfile is listed. CMake pins fetched C++ dependencies by tags, while FetchContent needs network access at configure time unless cached. Reproducibility needs lockfiles or constrained environments, hashes, compiler/toolchain documentation, and an offline build strategy."),
 ("What is one mismatch between the architectural claims and implementation worth discussing?", "The documentation advertises a full list of implemented features, but source reveals caveats: StopLimit is executed as Market; cancel does not remove dormant stop/peg metadata; replay does not apply cancel/execution events; and multi-asset routing retains stale IDs. I would explain each as current implementation status and a prioritized remediation rather than repeat feature claims unqualified."),
 ("How would you prioritize the top five engineering risks?", "First, enforce order validation and unique IDs to prevent book corruption. Second, fix cancellation/lifecycle across live, stop, peg, and symbol routing. Third, correct type semantics, especially StopLimit and auction/iceberg interactions. Fourth, harden numeric overflow and exception-safe insertion. Fifth, formalize single-writer concurrency and add model-based invariant tests. These priorities protect correctness before performance polish."),
 ("How would you make cancel/submit state changes auditable and recoverable?", "Assign every accepted command a monotonically increasing sequence number and append a validated command/result event to a durable log before publishing acknowledgments. Rebuild by deterministic replay into the same single-writer book, and make downstream fill delivery idempotent using sequence/fill IDs. The current engine has no persistent journal, so this is a design proposal, not an existing guarantee."),
 ("How would you redesign multi-asset concurrency for production?", "Give each symbol a single-writer actor or shard that owns its matching engine and consumes a bounded command queue. A router validates global IDs and routes commands, while cancel uses an authoritative live-order index with synchronized updates. Different symbols can process concurrently; commands for one symbol remain sequenced. Add queue backpressure, shutdown/drain semantics, and deterministic cross-symbol result ordering."),
 ("What is the strongest answer when an interviewer asks whether you personally fixed the bug?", "Be exact: ‘I found this defect while reviewing the checked-in implementation; this repository snapshot does not contain evidence of a completed fix, so I won’t claim I shipped one. The root cause is the index overwrite in `OrderBook::add`; my fix is to reject duplicate live IDs atomically, and I would validate it with a state-invariant regression and allocation-failure test.’ If I later implement it, I can update the answer with the actual diff and test result."),
 ("Close with a credible summary of your technical strengths and current limits.", "The project gives me concrete material to discuss matching algorithms, price-time queues, allocator lifetimes, atomics, wire formats, Python bindings, and stochastic simulation. I can explain the design choices and identify where code and documentation diverge. I should avoid claiming production-grade durability, complete exchange semantics, or measured low latency until the missing validation, lifecycle, recovery, and benchmark evidence exists. A strong engineer earns trust by naming those boundaries clearly."),
]),
]

